/**
 * @file mainpage.dox
 * @brief The documentation's main page (Doxygen only, not built).
 */

/**
 * @mainpage Sudoku for ESP32-P4
 *
 * A touch-screen Sudoku for the **Waveshare ESP32-P4-WIFI6-Touch-LCD-4B** (4-inch 720×720 MIPI-DSI
 * display, GT911 touch, ES8311 speaker), built with LVGL 9.6 as an Arduino sketch. These pages
 * document the code; how to play, the features and how to build are in the project's `docs/`
 * folder (and online at https://edipermadi.github.io/esp32p4_sudoku/docs/). These pages are online
 * too, with each release: https://edipermadi.github.io/esp32p4_sudoku/code/
 *
 * @section mp_what What the firmware does
 *
 * - **Puzzles**: 1600 built-in puzzles (the OpenSudoku collections), a generator for new ones at four
 *   levels, a daily puzzle, 63 variants besides Classic, puzzles typed in, imported from the card
 *   (81-digit lines or OpenSudoku's XML) or saved into collections of one's own; a search by name
 *   and variant, and a puzzle played before is known again even turned, mirrored or renumbered.
 * - **Playing**: pencil marks, Auto notes, undo/redo, a mistake check, step-by-step hints (up to
 *   X-Wing, Swordfish and XY-Wing), a Learn mode, starred puzzles, a 10-minute time attack, a tour
 *   of the game screen the first time; a puzzle shared as a QR code or sent to another board by
 *   sound.
 * - **Players and progress**: per-player statistics and emoji avatars, a history calendar, charts,
 *   achievements, daily and weekly leaderboards, records, insights into one's mistakes; every solve
 *   can be replayed or exported as an H.264 MP4.
 * - **Data**: everything kept in a SQLite database on the microSD card (`sudoku.db`), with CSV
 *   export, backups, a file manager with a Recycle bin, a USB drive mode and firmware updates from
 *   the card.
 * - **System**: a Wi-Fi clock with time zones, light and dark themes, sound effects, a lock screen,
 *   a screen saver (a dim digital or analog clock, the Game of Life, digital rain, strange attractors, boids, Lissajous figures, a double pendulum, a starfield, Langton's ant, Brian's Brain or other cellular automata, 2D or 3D), a system monitor, screenshots to the card, and remote control over the serial port (devctl) or
 *   by sound.
 *
 * @section mp_topics Where to start: the topics
 *
 * Each screen with logic of its own is split in two: the **screen** (`ui_*.c`: LVGL widgets,
 * drawing, taps) and its **core** (no LVGL calls: the state, the rules, sudoku.db and the card),
 * often a `.c` / `.h` per part. Both are bundled under a topic:
 *
 * | Topic | The screen, and what its core does |
 * |-------|------------------------------------|
 * | @ref topic_game | the game screen, hints, New puzzle, the daily puzzle, Learn, Share; the puzzle generator, solver and grader |
 * | @ref topic_puzzles | the collections, the grid of numbers or rows, Starred, search; selection, solved counts, the player's collections, the canonical hash, import parsing |
 * | @ref topic_continue | Home's Continue and the Unfinished games dialog; the current player's unfinished games |
 * | @ref topic_statistics | levels, the table and progress chart; the view from sudoku.db or RAM, totals, trends |
 * | @ref topic_history | the calendar and a day's games; the month asked for, the badges, the day picked |
 * | @ref topic_gameplays | recorded solves, the Replay screen, MP4 export; paging, the replay timeline, the video's frames |
 * | @ref topic_trophies | achievements, daily and weekly leaderboards, records; the day and week shown, the results |
 * | @ref topic_players | players, colours, the avatar picker; the list, switching, adding, renaming, deleting |
 * | @ref topic_files | the card's folders and files; reading a folder, rename, the Recycle bin, a puzzle file's preview |
 * | @ref topic_settings | the five Settings tabs; firmware update (@ref fw_update), the microSD card (@ref sd_card), factory reset (@ref device_ops), the USB drive (@ref usb_drive) |
 * | @ref topic_sysmon | the system monitor's tabs; the chip, CPU load, memory, storage, tasks, the last hour |
 * | @ref topic_lock | the lock screen and PIN setup; the PIN, the tries and the wait |
 *
 * Besides those:
 *
 * - @ref stats_db : the game's data in `sudoku.db`. One worker task does all database and card
 *   work; the UI queues commands and picks up the results (the tables are described there, and in
 *   `docs/internals/database.md`).
 * - @ref devctl : commands on the USB-UART port (screenshots, touches, files, SQL) for
 *   documentation and testing, driven by `tools/devctl.py`; @ref serial_port is Arduino's Serial
 *   for it, from C.
 * - @ref ui_home_menu : how Home lays out its menu buttons.
 * - @ref ui_sudoku : the UI's start-up, notices, the screen saver (Rain: @ref saver_rain_core; Attractor:
 *   @ref saver_attractor_core; Boids: @ref saver_boids_core; Lissajous: @ref saver_lissajous_core; Pendulum:
 *   @ref saver_pendulum_core; Starfield: @ref saver_stars_core; Game of Life, Brian's Brain, Langton's ant and the other cellular automata:
 *   @ref saver_automata_core; 3D automata: @ref saver_automata3d_core), and what devctl asks of it. Home
 *   is `ui_home.c`; what all the UI files share (the palette, layout, widgets, the game's state) is
 *   in `ui_internal.h` and `ui_common.c`.
 * - @ref media : the picture and video viewer, MP4 files read and written (replay export), and
 *   screenshots (@ref screenshot_core).
 * - @ref bsp : the board itself: display, touch, backlight, speaker and microphones, the microSD
 *   slot, power off.
 * - The RAM copy of the settings and statistics is in `store.cpp`, the `.sdx` puzzle format in
 *   `sdx.c`, Wi-Fi in `wifi_mgr.cpp` (its page: `ui_wifi.c`), sound effects in `sfx.c`, remote
 *   control by sound in `gibberlink.cpp`, the USB drive in `usb_msc.cpp`, SQLite's VFS in
 *   `sqlite/sqlite3_esp.c`, the time zones offered in Settings in `timezones.c`.
 *
 * @ref screens draws every screen and how a tap leads from one to another (Graphviz).
 *
 * @section mp_running How it runs
 *
 * - **LVGL** runs on core 1, from the sketch's `loop()`: every screen, and devctl's commands.
 * - **sudoku.db's worker** (stats_db.c) runs every query and write, one at a time; writes are
 *   queued and forgotten, reads are answered later and taken by each screen's poll.
 * - **Short worker tasks** on core 0 do the slow card work away from LVGL: reading a folder for
 *   Files, deleting for good, writing a screenshot, counting the card's free space, the task list
 *   for the system monitor.
 * - **The generator** makes puzzles on a worker core while the screen keeps going.
 *
 * Internal RAM (768 KB) is the tight resource and PSRAM (32 MB) is plenty: `malloc()` goes to
 * PSRAM, and big buffers are asked for there explicitly; only DMA and the video encoder need
 * internal RAM.
 *
 * @section mp_rules Conventions
 *
 * - **No `extern` variables**: every variable is `static` in the file that owns it; other files call
 *   its accessors. Constant tables too are read through a function.
 * - **Names**: a function that answers a yes/no question about state starts with `is_`
 *   (is_stats_db_ready()); UI files are `ui_*.c`; the code without LVGL is `*_core.c`, or a file per
 *   part under a `*_core.h` that includes them all.
 * - **Style**: `make format` (astyle, every if/for/while/else body in braces); `make format-check`
 *   says whether anything isn't formatted.
 * - **Documentation**: every function, parameter, return value and variable is documented; a
 *   function declared in a header is documented there, its definition says
 *   `(documented in X.h)`. `make doxygen` builds these pages and counts the warnings (kept at 0;
 *   they are in `build/doxygen/warnings.log`).
 *
 * @section mp_tests Testing
 *
 * - **On the computer**: `make test` builds `tests/` with AddressSanitizer and UBSan and runs it:
 *   the generator, solver and grader (with golden hashes to catch an unintended change), the
 *   built-in puzzles and their canonical hashes, the `.sdx` format, and the plain-C cores
 *   (@ref canon_core, @ref search_core, @ref learn_core, @ref game_core, @ref hint_core,
 *   @ref share_core, @ref daily_core, @ref import_parse, @ref saver_rain_core, @ref saver_attractor_core,
 *   @ref saver_boids_core, @ref saver_lissajous_core, @ref saver_pendulum_core, @ref saver_stars_core, @ref saver_automata_core, @ref saver_automata3d_core). Anything kept free
 *   of LVGL and the card
 *   can be added there.
 * - **On the board**: @ref devctl drives the real UI over USB-UART (touches, screenshots, files,
 *   SQL) from `tools/devctl.py`.
 *
 * @section mp_build Building
 *
 * | Command | What it does |
 * |---------|--------------|
 * | `make` | builds the firmware into `build/` |
 * | `make upload PORT=/dev/ttyACM0` | builds, then flashes it over the UART port |
 * | `make avatars PORT=/dev/ttyACM0` | flashes the emoji avatars into their partition (once per board) |
 * | `make test` | the tests of the plain-C parts on the computer: the generator, the built-in puzzles, the `.sdx` format |
 * | `make format` / `make format-check` | formats the C code / checks it is formatted |
 * | `make doxygen` | these pages, into `build/doxygen/html` |
 * | `make release VERSION=v1.2.3` | the release files into `dist/` |
 * | `make site` | the web flasher and docs site into `site/` |
 *
 * The full instructions (the libraries, `lv_conf.h`, the board settings, the partition table,
 * the web flasher) are in `docs/building.md`; the source layout and how to regenerate the
 * embedded data in `docs/development.md`.
 */
